,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+4(0) & =A(0+2)^{2}+B(0+2)+C \\\\ 0 & =4A+2B-4 \\\\ 2A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}+4(-1) & =A(-1+2)^{2}+B(-1+2)+C \\\\ -3 & =A+B-4 \\\\ A+B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the equations $2A+B=2$ and $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =1 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341168 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341169 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341170 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341171 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341172 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1041 "selection" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341169 13194139535275 true] [17592186341167 1110 #uuid "e6998aba-b4b4-542e-9a64-2e9319fb2473" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341168 1034 "\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535275 true] [17592186341168 1111 #uuid "a7e8e8af-06f2-5666-846f-0cc16e2ced70" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341169 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535275 true] [17592186341169 1111 #uuid "85e32b4c-4c20-5634-8c37-68e69ed9d7b5" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341170 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{4}{x+2}+K" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535275 true] [17592186341170 1111 #uuid "d649e9f3-a865-516b-aa83-6eab5c732fb4" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341171 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{4}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535275 true] [17592186341171 1111 #uuid "15c92157-27dd-5825-b36a-4434b38cd2f0" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341172 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|-\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535275 true] [17592186341172 1111 #uuid "7cfd4e20-c970-56ce-98c9-bef2e80b611b" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341173 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341174 13194139535275 true] [17592186341173 1089 "q-82895" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341173 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535275 true] [17592186341173 1100 "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341173 1113 #uuid "f4034c63-29b7-549e-ae42-2e9b3ad3f649" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341173 1145 "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+3=A(x-2)^{2}+B(x-2)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}-3(2)+3 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-3(1)+3 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=3$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{2}-3(3)+3 & =A(3-2)^{2}+B(3-2)+C \\\\ 3 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=0$ and $A+B=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =0+2 \\\\ 2A & =2 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nand substituting back into $A-B=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-B & =0 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{1}{x-2}-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341175 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341176 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341177 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341178 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341179 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1041 "selection" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341176 13194139535275 true] [17592186341174 1110 #uuid "bed091bf-5190-59da-86c2-96452e2159c6" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341175 1034 "-\\frac{1}{x-2}-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K" 13194139535275 true] [17592186341175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535275 true] [17592186341175 1111 